## Where settings live

| | Global config | `.prassets.yaml` |
|---|---|---|
| Location | `[[ .ConfigPath ]]` (`pra config path`) | the repo root, committed |
| Owner | the user, one per machine | the repo, shared with collaborators |
| Holds | stores (endpoint, bucket, public_url, credentials), the repo to store map (`repos:`, `default:`), the key prefix, optional `pr` and `types` overrides | `pr` and `types` only |
| Trust | trusted: credential templates can call `env`, `cmd` and `file` | untrusted: key templates get only `lower` and `slugify` |
| Schema | `pra schema global` | `pra schema repo` |

Types merge in this order, and a later layer wins per type name: the built-in `pr` type (`pr/{{ .Branch }}/{{ .File }}`), then `.prassets.yaml`, then the global entry. The prefix of the global entry always comes first in every key, so a repo file cannot write outside the area that the user gives the repo.

## Onboard a repository

1. Run `pra onboard` (this guide) and `pra config check` to see what is missing.
2. Global config. If it is missing, the user runs `pra config init` and fills in a store. Credentials are templates such as `'{{ env "AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY" }}'` or `'{{ cmd "op read op://Vault/item/field" }}'`. Never write a secret value into a config file.
3. Map the repo to a store. A repo without an entry uses `default:` when the config has one. To give the repo its own store or prefix, the user adds an entry to the global config:

   ```yaml
   repos:
     [[ if .Repo ]][[ .Repo ]][[ else ]]github.com/owner/name[[ end ]]:
       store: [[ if .Store ]][[ .Store ]][[ else ]]<store name>[[ end ]]
       prefix: "github/{{ .Owner }}/{{ .Repo }}/"
   ```

4. Define the layout. Without a repo file, the repo has only the `pr` type. To add types, commit `.prassets.yaml` at the repo root:

   ```yaml
   # yaml-language-server: $schema=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/hay-kot/prassets/main/schema.repo.json
   types:
     blog:
       key: "blog/{{ .Slug }}/{{ .File }}"
       usage: images for one blog post; the slug is the post's file name
     brand:
       key: "brand/{{ .File }}"
       usage: logos
   ```

   Key templates can use `.Host`, `.Owner`, `.Repo`, `.Branch` (slugified), `.Slug` and `.File`. `.File` must be the last segment. A type that uses `.Slug` needs `--slug` on put.
5. Check the result with `pra types` and `pra config check`. A failed step in `config check` names the problem.

## Rules for agents

- The global config belongs to the user and holds credentials. Change it only when the user asks. You can change `.prassets.yaml` like any other repo file.
- `.prassets.yaml` cannot set a store, a prefix or a secret. Strict decoding rejects those keys.
- Do not pass `--force` unless the user asks. Browsers and CDNs can keep the old copy of a key for up to a year, so upload under a new name instead.
- `pra rm` asks for confirmation only on a terminal. Without a terminal it deletes at once.
- Use `--format json` to parse output. stdout holds only results, and logs go to stderr. Exit code 2 means a usage error, so fix the command line. Exit code 1 means the operation failed.

## Daily use

```sh
pra put shot.png                         # upload to this branch's PR area; prints the URL
pra --format md put a.png b.png          # Markdown for a PR body
pra put -t blog -s my-post hero.webp     # another type
pra md                                   # Markdown for every asset of this branch
pra ls --all                             # everything under the repo prefix
```
